## Architecture: Event-Driven / Event Sourcing

```
Write: HTTP → Controller → Command Handler → Aggregate → Event Store → Outbox → Projection
Read:  HTTP → Controller → Projection (materialized view, never the event store)
```

- **Domain events are the source of truth** — no direct entity persistence
- **Event Store** — append-only immutable log
- **Aggregate** — rebuilds state from events, tracks uncommitted changes
- **Projection** — maintains denormalized read models; queries hit this directly
- **Outbox** — reliably stores events before publishing to a message broker

## Adding a new module

1. Create `src/features/<moduleName>/` with the following structure:
```
<moduleName>/
├── <moduleName>.routes.ts            ← composition root (wire everything here)
├── commands/
│   ├── create-<moduleName>.command.ts
│   └── create-<moduleName>.command-handler.ts
├── events/
│   ├── <moduleName>-created.event.ts
│   ├── <moduleName>-updated.event.ts
│   └── <moduleName>-deleted.event.ts
├── aggregates/
│   └── <moduleName>.aggregate.ts      ← pure domain logic, event replay
├── projections/
│   └── <moduleName>.projection.ts     ← materialized read model
├── event-store/
│   └── event-store.ts                ← append-only event log
├── dto/
│   └── create-<moduleName>.dto.ts
└── infrastructure/
    ├── <moduleName>.controller.ts
    └── outbox/
        └── outbox.ts                 ← reliable event publishing queue
```

2. Register the router in `src/app.ts`:
```ts
import { {{camelCase moduleName}}Router } from './features/<moduleName>/<moduleName>.routes';
// ...
app.use({{camelCase moduleName}}Router.routes());
app.use({{camelCase moduleName}}Router.allowedMethods());
```

## Koa specifics

- Use `ctx.body` to set response, `ctx.status` for HTTP status
- Access request body via `ctx.request.body` (parsed by koa-bodyparser)
- Route params via `ctx.params.id`
- Throw `AppError` / `NotFoundError` from `src/errors/app-error` — the error handler middleware catches them
- No `res.send()`, no `next()` callbacks — pure async/await

## Key rules

- All state changes MUST go through the event store
- Never mutate the projection from the write path — the handler updates projection after store append
- Aggregate version is used for optimistic concurrency control
- The Outbox pattern ensures events are never lost between store append and publishing
